## we need to use two functions to create device file automatically

1) 
## class_create()
* prototype
struct class * class_create(struct module *owner, const char *name);

1. owner – pointer to the module that is to “own” this struct class
2. name – pointer to a string for the name of this class

This will create the struct class for our device driver. It will create a structure under/sys/class/.
This is used to create a struct class pointer that can be used in calls to device_create().
Returns struct class pointer on success, or ERR_PTR() on error.
Note, the pointer created here is to be destroyed when finished by making a call to class_destroy().

2) 
## device_create()
* prototype
struct device *device_create(struct *class, struct device *parent, dev_t dev, void * drvdata, const char *fmt, ...);

1. class – pointer to the struct class that this device shousld be registered to
2. parent – pointer to the parent struct device of this new device, if any
3. devt – the dev_t for the char device to be added
4. drvdata – the data to be added to the device for callbacks
5. fmt – string for the device’s name
... – variable arguments

3)
## class_destroy()
* prototype
void class_destroy (	struct class * cls);

destroys a struct class structure

4)
## device_destroy()
* prototype
void device_destroy(struct class * class, dev_t devt);

removes a device that was created with device_create

## varification 

1) compile the driver
2) insert the kernel module using insmod
3) varify device file using ls -l /dev/my_device
4) remove the module using rmmod driver
